% ---------- Abstract ----------
\section{Abstract}
\hl{High-fidelity simulation of longitudinal train dynamics (LTD) captures nonlinear slack couplers, per-vehicle grade and curvature, and power-limited traction, but its computational cost — often tens of CPU-hours per scenario — limits its use for control design and operational planning that require repeated evaluation. This paper presents a train-agnostic, differentiable surrogate model for LTD intended to serve as the simulation backbone for fuel-optimal control of freight consists. The train is represented as a chain graph, with vehicles as nodes and couplers as edges, using a tensorized state representation that generalizes to arbitrary consist length without padding. A message-passing graph neural network learns a physics-residual correction to a known force-balance right-hand side and is integrated as a neural ODE, preserving the chain topology of the consist and remaining differentiable end to end. Training data are generated from a reference LTD simulator driven along real-terrain route profiles derived from elevation and track alignment data, with additional scenarios produced by perturbing real baselines in grade, adhesion, and train makeup. The surrogate is benchmarked against physics-only and physics-residual MLP baselines, and against a transformer-based variant, on trajectory accuracy, trip-level summaries (energy, arrival time, peak coupler force), and generalization to unseen consist sizes and corridors. We then use the surrogate as a differentiable world model for model-predictive control that minimizes trip energy subject to speed limits and coupler-force safety constraints. The surrogate is expected to reproduce reference-simulator trajectories at a fraction of the computational cost while generalizing across train length and route, enabling gradient-based control that reduces fuel consumption relative to a constant-speed baseline. These findings would suggest that graph-based, physics-informed surrogates offer a practical path toward scalable, energy-aware freight train control.}

\hfill\break%
\noindent\textbf{Objectives:}~Freight consists use massive amounts of energy to transport cargo cross-country, and even small inconsistencies in the control can cause massive fuel losses in the long run. A system model of the freight consist is needed for control experiments, but freight consists are both highly nonlinear and the dynamics varies across both consist composition and route profiles. A data-driven system identification is required, but a comprehensive train dynamics dataset that can generalize across consists and routes is hard to come by, if not unavailable completely. Thus, a simulation environment is needed for building and testing control laws across a family of consists and route profiles. We seek to build such an environment, and frame it as a data-driven system identification framework, modeling the family of trains as graphs and training a graph neural network to model the system dynamics, enabling control experiments to be carried out.  

\hfill\break%
\noindent\textbf{Methods:}~In 3--4 sentences, describe the study design and technical rigor. Identify data type(s), source(s), and scope (e.g., sample size, time frame, and geography). Summarize the analytical approach used to evaluate the objective.

\hfill\break%
\noindent\textbf{Findings:}~In 2--3 sentences, report the most important results. Indicate whether results are significant based on statistical testing, sensitivity analysis, validation, and/or rigorous qualitative assessment.

\hfill\break%
\noindent\textbf{Novelty:}~In 1--2 sentences, clearly state the novel transportation contribution of this work.

\hfill\break%
\noindent\textbf{Practical Applications:}~In 1--2 sentences, explain how findings inform transportation practice and/or policy, including specific implementation considerations or operational contexts.

